# Introduction

\label{ch:intro}

Can an embedded controller designed for use in large commercial HVAC 
units (NXP i.MX 8M Plus) power useful AI/ML inference and prediction
algorithms on the edge within acceptable time and resource limits? 
Is this control board, as currently designed, a feasible option for 
running AI and ML algorithms in its current state, or should another 
edge analytics platform design be explored? 

Imagine a scenario where a large commercial HVAC company lauches its
next gereration controller (Grpyhon for short). This controller has
a modern interface with a high resolution screen, and sophisticated
algorithms used for cooling and heating large commercial buildings.

This Gryphon controller has a System on Module (SOM) embedded
architecture integrated within this full scale custom controller.  
It is the modern embedded system device capable of controlling
cooling and heating on a large scale.

Quickly after this new controller is developed, ChatGPT and large
language models of AI is introduced and explodes on the scene
rapidly. Everyone begins to use AI and ML on a regular basis
following this introduction.

In this scenario, the fictional chief executive of said company
desires to become a part of this AI boom and asks a basic question.  
Seeing that there is a hardware develpment happening in a relatively
short timeframe from this AI boom, there are thoughts around making
them work together.

This thesis explores an important question: With all the problem solving
that artificial intelligence and machine learing is able to do,
is the current iMX8M Plus SOM controller board sufficient for this use,
or is a hardware upgrade necessary considering its limited processing
power, memory, and design for small embedded systems?

This feasibility study of the controller to run AI and ML algorithms 
would evaluate whether this device can meet certain time and 
completion restraints to provide for learning, infrernce, and 
prediction with varied small, nominal, and large datasets representing 
years worth of runtime data. 

The impact of AI on society in 2026 cannot be overstated --- nearly
everyone in the country has heard of it but less than three years
ago it was hardly a household name. This technological tidal wave is
washing over industry and moving very quickly. The impact is directly
observable by users of consumer search engines, people in tech jobs,
and computer users in general. 

Less obvious is how AI has crept into embedded systems where the
computer's role is behind-the-scenes. This means many industries, 
such as HVAC manufacturers and servicing, have to be nimble to
accommodate and adapt. This is especially challenging because control 
boards and in-house developed embeddedsystems have long development 
cycles. This thesis considers one specific platform and the 
feasibility hosting AI capabilities on an existing HVAC control 
system in production now.

There has been a shift in architecture as the industry is moving 
from centralized, cloud-only AI to a hybrid approach where "smarter" 
local compute is used for rapid, real-time decisions, leaving heavy 
training in the cloud.

There have been hardware advancements. The rise of specialized 
hardware accelerators—such as MCUs with built-in neural network
engines—enables sophisticated models to run on battery-powered
sensors.

Some key drivers shoudl be considered.  The main reasons for moving 
inference to the edge include reducing network traffic, maintaining 
high data privacy, and providing instantaneous responses for 
applications; like with HVAC units in specific cooling spaces

By building a proper runtime environment using Yocto and added
recipes examining how the board handles tasks, uses energy, and
connects with AI tools, this study aims to find out if the iMX8M
Plus is a suitable option for using AI in places where computing
resources are scarce, and whether it can perform well enough
compared to stronger computers.

The study plans to experiment with a few different scenarios for
testing including using a Linear Regression model with Gradient
Descent curves both using the IMX board and comparing that timing
output with a laptop computer. The AI runtimes are a critical measure
of the suitibility of the sytem in general.

The IMX8M Plus board also comes with a GPU. Future work can include
performing experiments to see the timing, CPU, Energy, Memory usage
when using the GPU and also without using build in GPU.

To answer this thesis question, the research will use a
step-by-step method by adapting simple AI tools to work on the
iMX8M Plus board, using its main processors to speed up predictions.
The plan involves testing how well AI models perform by measuring
things like execution speed, and comparing these results to
how the same models run on stronger computers, like laptop, and
for future work, graphics cards.

The first point in helping to answer this question will be to
determine if the programs used in our Intro to ML class can
actually run on the target embedded system. Initially the
board is developed using a version of Ubuntu linux built
with Yocto recipes but python is not natively installed as
a recipy on the board.

The second point will be to determin how effectively can
a sample of assignment problems from an Intro to ML class
taken at UNCC graduate level can run on both the target device
as well as a standard laptop. There are a few projects and
assignments that may help to answer this question.

Several specific tests will be done: first,
using linear regression with gradient descent to train a model
and verify its output using supervision, second computing cost
functions using variables and Theta changes, third Predictive
models using both Training and Test data, Clasisification
problems with predictions and accuracy scoring.

The third point will be to also include diffferent sizes of
datasets to better simlate real world sizes of data rather
than just the nominal sizes from the Intro To ML class.
Changing certain parameters will also be testing to vary
the size and speed the model will perform teh algorithms
to see if t's possible to teach small AI networks right on
the board.

The fourth point will be to run an different model
altogether and determine if this is feasible task. The
MicroGPT algoritm was chosen for this effort. Using the
MicroGPT model, a python script based largly on
ChatGPT without any outside depnendncies, can simulate
runnging on an air gapped system as long as an input
file is provided.

These tests along with changes in parameters and inputs
will provide real-world proof to show if the iMX8M Plus can
handle useful AI applications on small devices.